[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket and its own fix.
</Callout>

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on #4471 writes the
  day first, so a date with day 12 or lower comes out swapped. Any channel, every tenant.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date the
  tenant's way, day first. People type what the hint says and get a swapped date. Only the
  `form` channel, only booking-v3 tenants. Fixing the parser does not fix this.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, it does not swap. "Tuesday showed as Monday."
  "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." This looks like time zones.
  The cause is not confirmed. The desk already noted these reports (`desk-notes.json`), but
  `causes.ts` files them under `parser` by tenant.

## How this was counted

- The export pages overlap, so some reports appear twice. Each report counts once, by id.
- `parser` and `hint` come from `causes.ts`: tenant locale, booking form and channel. Not the
  intake tag, which is a guess from the reporter's browser.
- `one-day` is every report in the desk's one-day list. Its body names a shift, not a swap.

## Why the merge missed it

Reports were merged on subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
All three problems produce the same subjects: "Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong". The
subject says that a date is wrong. Only the body, the tenant and the channel say why.

